The five grams of pure iron were put into an MgO crucible along with slag tablets. The sample crucible was suspended using a molybdenum wire, while a measured amount of aluminum encapsulated in pure iron foil and secured to a pure iron rod with pure iron wire was positioned at the upper section inside the reaction tube. A high flow rate (1500 ml/min) of pure argon (6 N) was introduced into the reaction tube for 10 min to reduce the oxygen level in the atmosphere, and then the argon flow rate was adjusted to 200 ml/min. The sample crucible was descended slowly to the hot zone (1873 K) of the furnace (about 15 min to reach 66 cm) to prevent the crucible from cracking due to thermal shock. After holding for 30 min to homogenize the slag melt, aluminum was immersed into the melt. After a reaction time of 60 min, which was chosen to resemble the ladle refining time, the crucible was quickly taken out from the furnace, and then while the outside of the crucible was dipped in water, the slag surface was rapidly quenched by blowing pure helium gas. The chemical composition of slag and metal samples were characterized by SEM–EDS (Model: Hitachi TM4000Plus) for microstructural features and composition. In determining the slag composition, suspended metal particles were excluded from the measuring area. The weight of the slag after the experiment, SW f , was calculated from the CaO mass balance, as shown in Eq.  2 . 2 Weight of final slag ( SW f ) = 1 ( mass\% CaO ) f × mass\%CaO i · SW i - mass\%Ca f · MW f · 56 / 40 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
